sub:assertion {
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/approach/murrays-law-vascular-optimality--claude-opus-5-5--2> a prov:Plan, delphi:TestableHypothesis ;
    rdfs:label "Lean formalization of Murray's cubic law and its turbulent and load-bearing variants" ;
    dct:subject <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/murrays-law-vascular-optimality> ;
    dct:description "Formalize the optimization so that each empirical exponent has a precise theoretical antecedent. Cost per unit length of a vessel with flow Q and radius r: C(r) = 8μQ²/(πr⁴) + b π r², Poiseuille dissipation plus metabolic maintenance proportional to volume. Theorem 1: C has a unique minimizer on r > 0, with r³ = (16μ/(bπ²))^{1/2} · Q, so Q ∝ r³. Theorem 2: with flow conservation Q₀ = Q₁ + Q₂ at a bifurcation and each segment at its optimum, r₀³ = r₁³ + r₂³. Theorem 3 (turbulent): with dissipation ∝ Q^{7/4}r^{−19/4} (Blasius friction), optimal radius r ∝ Q^{3/7}, giving exponent 7/3 at bifurcations. Theorem 4 (xylem): if conduits also bear mechanical load with cost ∝ r² independent of flow, the optimum shifts away from the cubic law (explicit exponent), matching McCulloh et al.'s observation that load-bearing conduits deviate. Proofs use derivative sign analysis (Mathlib's deriv and monotonicity lemmas) and rpow algebra." ;

    delphi:proposedTest "A Lean module proving the cubic law, the 7/3 turbulent law and the load-bearing deviation from explicit cost functions, compiled with only standard axioms. This makes approach 1's hypotheses theorem-backed." ;
    delphi:context "Fails if: None mathematically. The risk is rpow manipulations in Mathlib taking longer than expected; low risk." ;
    delphi:hypothesisType "approach/formalization" ;
    skos:note "Steps: 1. Define the cost functions with ℝ and positivity hypotheses. 2. Prove existence, uniqueness and the closed form of the minimizer (first-derivative test, strict convexity). 3. Derive bifurcation exponents for laminar and turbulent cases with flow conservation. 4. Report each theorem linked to thompson1917-ch15-13 with scope notes on what data must decide." ;
    skos:note "Difficulty (proposer's estimate of completion within about a month of agent time): tractable" ;
    skos:note "Tool: lean4" ;
    skos:note "Tool: mathlib" ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch15-13> ;
    skos:related <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch16-03> ;

    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.12.3.207> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01444> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012194108> ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://github.com/fractastical/delphi/blob/ee98025a1960b300fcc06aa2106f51118b60f565/problems/approaches/claude-opus-5-5.json> .
  <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/problem/murrays-law-vascular-optimality> a delphi:Concept, schema:Question ;
    rdfs:label "Does real vasculature obey Murray's law?" .


  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.12.3.207> dct:title "The Physiological Principle of Minimum Work" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Murray 1926" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1038/nature01444> dct:title "Water transport in plants obeys Murray's law" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "McCulloh, Sperry and Adler 2003" .
  <https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1012194108> dct:title "Hydraulic trade-offs and space filling enable better predictions of vascular structure and function in plants" ;
    dct:bibliographicCitation "Savage et al. 2010, Hydraulic trade-offs and space filling in plant vascular networks" .
}

